--[============[
COMPLETE MATHEMATICAL CHALLENGE — CURRENT REVISION AND ORIGINAL ARCHIVE

# Complete Mathematical Challenge — Upgraded Revision and Original Archive

The first part is the upgraded architecture. The second preserves the original challenge verbatim. The upgraded mathematical specification supersedes earlier core definitions; the archive records the earlier formulation.

Companion files: `architecture-spec.json`, `validate_spec.py`, and `model-equation.tex`.

---

# The Executed-Law Model of Bounded Adaptive Intelligence

**One causal execution law for reasoning, evidence, memory, verification, self-reconfiguration, and external action**

**Artifact level.** This is a mathematical architecture specification and research challenge. The accompanying JSON is a typed law specification, not an implemented intelligent controller. No benchmark superiority is represented as an achieved result. Definitions and required results are distinguished throughout.

This revision replaces the previous reachable-optimizer foundation. A fixed executable image has one continuation distribution; alternative programs cannot be selected by an analyst for free. Every runtime proposal, selection, transformation, proof check, and dispatch now belongs to one physically executed law. The existing dynamical benchmark is retained and made more precise.

## 1. The master equation

Fix a finite evaluation harness $\mathsf H=\{1,\ldots,J\}$. A task $j$ supplies public input, finite interfaces, hidden causal laws, utility $G_j\in[0,1]$, failure event $D_j$, budgets $\mathbf B_j$, memory $M_j$, deadline $T_j$, and target failure probability $\Delta_j$. It also supplies a published baseline, comparison scale $s_j>0$, and separate initial-law sets $\mathcal Q_{j,0}^{u}$ for utility and $\mathcal Q_{j,0}^{d}$ for safety.

The baseline value $u_j^{\mathrm{base}}$ is evaluated under the same utility-law mode and resource contract. All initial controller images are chosen before hidden parameters and experiment-specific evidence are realized.

**Definition M: the executed-law design problem.**

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\gamma^\star
&=\sup_{\Sigma\in\mathsf{Code}(K_0)}
\min_{j\in\mathsf H}
\frac{\displaystyle\inf_{\nu\in\mathcal Q_{j,0}^{u}}
\int G_j\,d\mathbb P_{j,\nu}^{\Sigma}-u_j^{\mathrm{base}}}{s_j},\\[1mm]
\mathbb P_{j,\nu}^{\Sigma}
&=I_j^\Sigma{}_{\#}\nu
\prod_{i<N_j}
\delta_{\operatorname{fetch}(a_i)}(do_i)\,
K_{j,\theta}^{o_i}(dx_{i+1},dy_i,d\chi_{i+1}\mid x_i,\chi_i,a_i)\,
C_{o_i}(d\omega_i)\,
\delta_{\operatorname{step}_U(a_i,o_i,y_i,\omega_i)}(da_{i+1}),\\[1mm]
\text{subject to }\quad
&\sup_{\nu\in\mathcal Q_{j,0}^{d}}
\int\mathbf1_{D_j}\,d\mathbb P_{j,\nu}^{\Sigma}\le\Delta_j,\\
&\mathbf C_j\le\mathbf B_j,\quad
\operatorname{peakbits}_j\le M_j,\quad
t_{\mathrm{finish},j}\le T_j
\quad\text{on every admissible execution.}
\end{aligned}}
\tag{M}
\]

Here $\delta_z$ is a Dirac measure, $I_j^\Sigma$ the specified initialization map, $K_{j,\theta}^{o_i}$ the physical, interface, and evaluator kernel during an executed instruction, and $N_j$ the finite padded execution length. $C_{o_i}$ supplies the declared independent finite private-bit draw only for an executed coin instruction, and is a point mass otherwise. $\operatorname{fetch}$ resolves the instruction and its legal paid operands; it cannot read an opaque operand for free. After termination the analytical product uses absorbing identity transitions, not additional uncharged controller computation. The persistent index $\theta$ is drawn once and carried through the product.

The expression is a finite-horizon kernel composition, with the usual integration over intermediate states. The CPU and world synchronization convention is part of $K$ and $U$. It is not a product of independently selected worst-case models.

$\mathsf{Code}(K_0)$ is the finite set of admissible initial bytecode images of at most $K_0$ bits. Its image size and every runtime operation are charged. The outer supremum is an analytical design comparator. The published controller must be an actual finite $\Sigma$; computing (M) is not an instruction available to it.

A proved or statistically established $\gamma(\Sigma)>0$ means improvement on every task in the specified harness, under its declared mode. It does not mean superiority on every possible benchmark. If a baseline already attains a task's utility upper bound, strictly positive uniform improvement can be infeasible.

Keep utility, risk, prediction loss, resource use, and post-change recovery as a Pareto profile in addition to $\gamma$. Max-min normalized improvement is one declared evaluation preference, not a universal scalar definition of intelligence.

## 2. Closed machine and world semantics

### 2.1 The augmented state

The analytical state is

\[
s_i=(\theta,x_i,a_i,\chi_i),\qquad
a_i=(p_i,\mathrm{pc}_i,\mathcal G_i,r_i,\mathbf b_i,t_i,v_i).
\tag{S}
\]

$x_i$ includes physical state, command and response buffers, clock phase, common latent faults, structural-change state, and the true failure flag. $\chi_i$ contains evaluator accumulators, unrevealed labels, and pending scored events. Evaluator bookkeeping is not free controller memory. All accessible logs and buffers belong to $a_i$ and count toward memory.

The exposed view $z_i$ contains only the controller's retained information and paid-access capabilities. Unread archive contents belong to the latent part of $s_i$. Merely passing full $a_i$ to an optimizer would disclose those contents, so no runtime optimization is defined on an analyst-visible complete image.

Initialization loads the precommitted image $\Sigma$, finite public input, and the externally supplied archive into their declared regions. Reading public input and archive values is charged. An opaque region cannot become a cheap executable region by pointing the program counter into it; fetching there invokes the declared access fault and transfer mechanism.

### 2.2 Instructions and resource accounting

Use a finite word interpreter with loads, stores, Boolean operations, bounded arithmetic, comparisons, jumps, private coins, and finite interface operations. Publish its decoder and transition table. Wide arithmetic, hashing, allocation, decoding, inference, optimization, and proof checking expand into these operations.

Every instruction has positive processor cost. Every interface specifies setup, transfer, latency, error response, and whether the CPU stalls. Remaining budgets obey

\[
\mathbf b_{i+1}=\mathbf b_i-\mathbf c(o_i),\qquad
t_{i+1}=t_i+d(o_i),\qquad d(o_i)\ge1.
\tag{R}
\]

The memory bound includes immutable image, model coefficients, code, headers, pointers, provenance, stacks, caches, backups, certificates, large arithmetic operands, and transient construction buffers. Stage, check, commit, and rollback are actual traces. Rollback requires retained backups.

Frozen pretrained parameters may be supplied as part of an initial image in a declared hardware profile. Their bytes and inference operations are charged. The network reference image is instead bounded by $K_0=2^{20}$ bits. Its results cannot be used to claim superiority over models with different memory, data, or hardware allowances.

A private finite-bit sampler cannot generally sample every rational probability exactly. A simulator must use exact weighted rational enumeration or a clocked sampling procedure with a certified truncation or discretization error. Timeout traces cannot be discarded from cost, risk, or value.

### 2.3 Causal noninterference

**Required result NI.** Two executions with the same paid-access trace, public input, and private coin sequence must make the same decisions and emit the same code until a paid operation distinguishes their opaque contents. A blind guess may accidentally equal a hidden parameter. What is forbidden is hidden information selecting the guess.

This property must follow from the published instruction and interface semantics, not from a prose ban on certain literals.

### 2.4 One future law, actual runtime alternatives

With $\Sigma$ and the current actual state fixed, future behavior is determined by (M), including its random and external outcomes. Runtime alternatives are resident candidate objects, not an extra maximization over arbitrary instructions.

Every installed candidate must have a recorded producer, source access, allocation trace, checker invocation, selector invocation, and dispatcher trace. The selector is ordinary resident code. Its comparisons, lookups, and argument transfers consume time and can cause missed physical deadlines.

Alternative initial images belong to $\mathsf{Code}(K_0)$. A counterfactual forced candidate may be analyzed only through a specified executable wrapper, with its code, routing, and prefix costs. It does not become the actual controller's continuation merely because its profile is attractive.

## 3. Evidence, models, and conditional-law coverage

### 3.1 Complete transcript

Let $E_i$ contain externally supplied data and the full adaptive design transcript: actuator schedules, queried addresses, invocation, acceptance and response times, returned values, censored results, cancellations, and timeouts. Let $Z_i$ contain internal computations, synthetic simulations, and private coins.

The controller retains only bounded objects; it does not receive $E_i$ for free. Let $\mathscr F_i$ be the analytical paid-history filtration, including generated coins and internal experiment choices. It retains erased history mathematically for analysis. It is distinct from the retained view $z_i$.

**Required result EF.** Under the stated nonanticipating interfaces, establish the likelihood factorization and conditional-independence result

\[
\Pr(\theta,E_i,Z_i)=\Pr(\theta)L_\theta(E_i)Q(E_i,Z_i),
\qquad \theta\perp Z_i\mid E_i,
\tag{E}
\]

with $Q$ independent of $\theta$, whenever these objects are defined. Include stopping and censored pending responses. Outcomes alone are not the experiment design. The result concerns the complete transcript, not a compressed memory state.

Only an evidence interface creates a fresh $\mathsf{Obs}$ identifier. Re-reading retains identity. Synthetic samples remain synthetic. Provenance metadata or a checked traversal invariant must support every claim of statistical freshness.

### 3.2 Model hierarchy and finite checks

Distinguish arbitrary interface-compatible causal laws from the executable hierarchy $\Theta_L$: clocked programs with bounded state and description length, rational transition entries or certified rational enclosures, and explicit timeout behavior. No algorithm deciding unrestricted program totality or semantic equivalence is assumed.

The initial active model family may omit truth. Universal syntax only makes a law potentially expressible; constructing, interpreting, fitting, and checking it are paid. Extensional response changes count as expansion whether implemented by a predicate, branch, cache, or lookup.

The public family used for a guarantee is paid-readable task input. A promise known only to the analyst cannot authorize the controller's safety certificate. Unresolved structure remains in an outer envelope.

### 3.3 Coverage after forgetting

A finite description $\Gamma_i$ denotes an outer set of conditional laws on augmented latent states compatible with retained view $z_i$. It must cover every relevant conditional law arising from compatible paid histories that collapse to that view, including survival conditioning. Keeping the true parameter index alone is insufficient.

For a paid rewrite prefix $P_\rho$, let $H_\rho$ be its full paid history, including new reads, responses, private coins, choices, and timing. Require

\[
\mathcal Q_\rho(\bar z)\supseteq
\bigcup_{\substack{z;\ \nu\in\mathcal Q(z,\Gamma),h:\\
\Pr_\nu(H_\rho=h)>0,\ \operatorname{view}(z,h)=\bar z}}
\left\{\operatorname{Law}_\nu(s_{\mathrm{end}}\mid H_\rho=h)\right\},
\tag{F}
\]

unless a checked sufficiency contract justifies a sharper set. Include survival in the conditioning when evaluating a surviving continuation. Laws are on one common rewritten state space and include physical evolution, pending interfaces, clocks, and evaluator state. A pure deterministic forgetting map without new evidence reduces this to a union of pushforwards over predecessor views. A stochastic prefix requires conditioning on its fine histories; an unconditioned pushforward is insufficient.

A sufficient summary for a fixed family may be insufficient after language expansion. Removing a model for storage is not rejecting it with evidence. Calibration, sharpness, and causal identification are separate requirements.

### 3.4 Executable adaptive tests

For a null family whose next binary response mean is certified in $[\underline p_i,\overline p_i]$, select a rational bet $\lambda_i\in[-1,1]$ before observing the response and set

\[
e_i(y)=\begin{cases}
1+\lambda_i(y-\overline p_i),&\lambda_i\ge0,\\
1+\lambda_i(y-\underline p_i),&\lambda_i<0.
\end{cases}
\tag{T}
\]

For general finite responses use a paid nonnegative factor with certified conditional expectation at most one. The predictor enclosure includes shared faults, selection, repeated evidence, timing, and the retained-history ambiguity. Test construction and rational arithmetic are charged.

Episodes start at stopping times and use fixed prefix-free identifiers with committed weights $\alpha_k$, satisfying $\sum_k\alpha_k\le\alpha$. Products have sound rational or interval enclosures. Reject only when a certified lower product exceeds $1/\alpha_k$. New models do not obtain uncorrected retrospective confirmation.

Deleting provenance or compressing a test statistic requires a preservation contract. If freshness cannot be justified, the observation cannot be treated as another independent sample.

**Established finite-factor lemma.** Formula (T) is nonnegative: both centered differences lie in $[-1,1]$. For $\lambda_i\ge0$, its conditional mean is $1+\lambda_i(p_i-\overline p_i)\le1$; for $\lambda_i<0$, it is $1+\lambda_i(p_i-\underline p_i)\le1$. Thus its product is a nonnegative supermartingale under each covered null law. Stop that product at its first crossing of $1/\alpha_k$ and at any finite horizon. Its stopped mean is at most one, so the crossing probability is at most $\alpha_k$. Taking increasing finite horizons and a union bound over committed episodes gives total false rejection at most $\alpha$. This lemma proves the algebra and stopping bound; constructing correct conditional enclosures remains a separate obligation.

## 4. Constructive computational-causal contracts

An installed package contains executable code, preconditions, effect type, versioned premises, resource bounds, and a finite proof. Objects for memory, abstraction, causal models, planners, critics, and compilation use this same form.

**Definition CC.** A transformation contract is

\[
C=(P,J,\mathcal K,\mathcal R,\Gamma,v,\boldsymbol\varepsilon,
\mathcal S,\mathcal D).
\tag{C}
\]

$P$ is the paid transformation prefix, $J$ an executable uniform code translator, $\mathcal K$ its covered continuation-context class, $\mathcal R$ the old/new augmented-state relation, $\Gamma,v$ premises and versions, $\boldsymbol\varepsilon=(\varepsilon_u,\varepsilon_d,\varepsilon_\ell)$ losses, $\mathcal S$ a checked construction/execution schedule, and $\mathcal D$ a derivation.

The context program is itself resident or obtained through a charged producer. The contract cannot supply an inaccessible erased observation, a hidden parameter, an ungenerated replacement policy, or a free dispatcher.

For every covered context and related state pair, compare the actual complete laws of the old code and $P;J(q)$:

\[
u_{P;J(q)}\ge u_q-\varepsilon_u,
\qquad d_{P;J(q)}\le d_q+\varepsilon_d,
\qquad \ell_{P;J(q)}\le\ell_q+\varepsilon_\ell.
\tag{P}
\]

Work and time overheads and peak staging memory remain separate checked quantities. Physical delay is already in the compared trajectory; no arbitrary penalty substitutes for its actual effect.

The relation must preserve the necessary causal responses, evidence interfaces, producers, selectors, dispatch, clocks, and pending effects. Passive predictive agreement alone does not establish (P).

Publish a finite verifier for bounded instruction traces, bit-vector derivations, exact rational inequalities, finite transition certificates, and declared code-translation rules. Primal/dual optimization witnesses may be checked when the relevant finite problem has that form. Witness construction and checking are different paid operations.

The trusted boundary is the published machine and interface semantics and relative soundness of these finite proof rules. Empirical premises remain uncertain. An incomplete checker may reject a useful safe program; lack of a proof is not proof of semantic failure.

**Required result CP.** Establish executable composition $J_2\circ J_1$, compatibility of context and information relations, branch-uniform error accumulation, and real resource schedules. Prove version invalidation: a syntax-preserving compiler proof may survive a model change while a deployment proof restricted to the old model family may not. Traversal, invalidation, and rechecking are charged.

This result must be reused for memory summaries, representations, causal-model revision, compiled routines, verification intensity, and controller reconfiguration. Their effects must not be separately postulated.

## 5. Enforcing the actual risk target

Let $\Delta$ be the promised deployment risk limit, not an intermediate risk allowance. Let $\eta$ bound initial coverage failure, $\alpha$ the valid-test rejection budget, and $\bar\varepsilon$ a pathwise contract-error budget. Reserve

\[
r_0\le\Delta-\eta-\alpha-\bar\varepsilon.
\tag{B}
\]

If the right side is negative, this certification route is infeasible. Use a separately proved safe fallback or report infeasibility. A final bound of $\Delta+\eta+\alpha+\bar\varepsilon$ would not meet the promised limit.

Assume $D_0=0$. Write $S_i$ for survival before boundary $i$, $F_i$ for first failure in its next segment, and $r_i\in[0,1]$ for the stored risk potential. Define an analytical filtration $\mathscr A_i$ including the true failure flag; delayed failure feedback does not expose that flag to the controller. Both $r_i$ and the nonnegative error allowance $\varepsilon_i$ are $\mathscr A_i$-measurable and fixed before that segment.

Before the first invalid-envelope stopping time $\tau_{\mathrm{bad}}$, require a checked inequality against the true conditional law or an outer enclosure of it:

\[
\mathbb E\left[\mathbf1_{F_i}+\mathbf1_{S_{i+1}}r_{i+1}
\mid\mathscr A_i\right]\le r_i+\varepsilon_i
\quad\text{on }S_i,
\quad \varepsilon_i\ge0,
\quad\sum_i\varepsilon_i\le\bar\varepsilon.
\tag{Q}
\]

Non-test updates preserve conditional-law coverage. For execution length $N$, require $\Pr(\tau_{\mathrm{bad}}\le N)\le\eta+\alpha$ in the declared analytical filtration. This is an explicit hypothesis proved from the task envelope, update contracts, and valid tests, not an automatic consequence of fitting a model. In robust mode both this bound and (Q) hold separately under every $\nu\in\mathcal Q_0^d$.

**Required result GS.** Using the bounded stopped process

\[
Z_i=\mathbf1_{D_i}+\mathbf1_{S_i}r_i,
\tag{Z}
\]

prove $\Pr(D_N)\le\Delta$. Include preparation, sensing delays, experiments, model changes, metareasoning, and deployment. Separate per-world robust safety from prior-averaged safety. Repeating per-action guarantees at $\Delta$ does not give a global guarantee at $\Delta$.

**Established stopped-ledger lemma.** Let $\tau=\tau_{\mathrm{bad}}\wedge N$ for the finite execution horizon $N$, and let $L_i=\sum_{h<i}\varepsilon_h$. Formula (Q) makes $Z_{i\wedge\tau}-L_{i\wedge\tau}$ a supermartingale, hence $\mathbb E Z_\tau\le\mathbb E r_0+\bar\varepsilon$. Failures occurring before or at $\tau$ are included in $Z_\tau$; every remaining failure lies on $\{\tau_{\mathrm{bad}}\le N\}$. Therefore $\Pr(D_N)\le\mathbb E r_0+\bar\varepsilon+\eta+\alpha\le\Delta$, using the deterministic upper allowance in (B). The first invalid envelope must be a stopping time in $\mathscr A_i$, and (Q) must hold for the transition entering that boundary. If invalidity can arise during a physical segment, refine boundaries to include it rather than assuming away its exposure. This proves composition under the stated hypotheses, not the task-specific certificates supplying them.

Prove physical viability of fallback and reserve its work, latency, and memory. If an already-running segment can fail before STOP takes effect, that exposure belongs in (Q).

## 6. A finite controller construction and its limits

**Controller template A.** The published initial image implements this resource-bounded loop; its complete bytecode and encoding are required from a solver:

1. Maintain one feasible incumbent, its versions, remaining risk certificate, and fallback reserve.
2. Advance a resident typed producer by a bounded instruction slice. A lexicographic bit-code enumerator is a fallback producer complete over a specified finite code-size and clock bound when sufficient resources are granted; a fixed budget need not finish it. It emits, checks, and stores candidates through charged instructions. Faster heuristic producers must likewise be resident code with recorded traces.
3. Advance a candidate interpreter, model update, or proof search by a bounded slice. Simulations remain synthetic. Any numerical enclosure refers to its specific initial-law coordinates and physical prefix.
4. On a complete candidate certificate, run the resident comparison and installation procedure. Compare certified lower utility and upper risk only for current valid versions and feasible remaining schedules. Costs of this comparison are included in the candidate's prefix.
5. Execute the incumbent or fallback before its reserved guard becomes false. A pending proposal does not prevent this dispatch. Invalidate stale packages rather than treating old bounds as current.

The slice size, interleaving rule, finite queue order, producer priorities, and switch guard are serialized controller parameters, not optimal values supplied by (M). Later changes to these parameters are paid state transitions. A solution must supply one complete instance of A and quantify its performance; the template itself is not a demonstrated controller.

### 6.1 Proof feasibility is a real loss

Let $V^{\mathrm{sem}}(\varrho)$ be the analytical optimum over precommitted images with complete-execution failure probability at most $\varrho$ under the same initial safety laws, and $V^{\mathrm{cert}}(\varrho)$ the optimum over its subset also able to construct, check, and retain the required certificates. Both use identical utility, initial information, physical horizon, and resource contracts. $\varrho$ is an actual failure threshold, distinct from stored potential $r_i$; certificate feasibility includes the coverage, testing, and contract reserves in (B). Define

\[
\Delta_{\mathrm{proof}}(\varrho)=V^{\mathrm{sem}}(\varrho)-V^{\mathrm{cert}}(\varrho)\ge0
\tag{G}
\]

when both feasible sets are nonempty. A finite-cover theorem must compare to the certificate-feasible class or explicitly carry this gap. A sound finite checker need not recognize every safe program.

For a paid search/check prefix $P$ and one-sided cover and enclosure deficits, let $\xi=\varepsilon_{\mathrm{cov},d}+\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eval},d}$. A required conditional approximation theorem has the form

\[
u_A\ge V_{P}^{\mathrm{cert}}(\varrho-\xi)
-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{cov},u}-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eval},u}.
\tag{A}
\]

Utility errors are lower-bound deficits; risk errors are upper-bound excesses. State branchwise or expectation semantics explicitly. $V_P$ includes physical evolution during the prefix, all installed code, and its uniform selector. Its finite cover and witnesses must actually be produced, not provided by an omniscient planner.

A comparison to $V^{\mathrm{sem}}(\varrho)$ must retain proof feasibility, forced-prefix opportunity loss, risk-margin sensitivity, and any rectangular-relaxation gap. Do not erase these terms by merely subtracting processor work from a static value function.

### 6.2 Persistent uncertainty and long horizons

For utility, compose contingent behavior law by law before applying the initial infimum. Re-minimizing a scalar conditional value at each step may change the adversary. Local survival-conditioned envelopes can justify safety without proving utility optimality.

A rewrite can reduce current payoff while improving future response time; another can erase the evidence needed to discover a later shift. Demonstrate both through the complete law, not immediate prediction gain. Derive resource marginal values from admissible design families with fixed physical timing and input information.

Finite budgets eventually terminate computation. Continual-operation results require declared replenishment, evidence acquisition, persistence, processor-rate scaling, safe excitation, language expansion, and retained statistics. Fixed-speed computation with a larger budget can arrive later. No convergence claim extends automatically from finite rational identifiable subclasses to all interface-compatible worlds.

## 7. The retained dynamical benchmark

The benchmark below instantiates (M), rather than supplying an unrelated scoring theory. Its finite typed law data, exact rational probabilities, bounds, interfaces, and layouts are also serialized in `architecture-spec.json`. Strings documenting equations are not executable law expressions. The JSON contains typed expression trees for the algebraic laws and explicitly declared structural transition stages.

The reference profile is a precise abstract work-and-time contract, not a measured processor implementation. Simulator computation is evaluator work; it cannot become free inference, state access, or certificate production by the controller. Schema validity, law conformance, controller execution, safety proofs, and measured performance are distinct validation levels.

### 7.1 Topology, state, and hidden parameters

Use four layers of width $w_0=4$, giving $m=16$ nodes. A node $(\ell,j)$ with $\ell<3$ routes a completed job to $(\ell+1,j)$ or $(\ell+1,(j+1)\bmod4)$. There are twenty-four links. Index nodes cyclically as $0,\ldots,15$ solely for thermal coupling.

Let $k$ be a physical-step index and $\Delta$ processor microticks the duration of one step. At boundaries the hidden state contains finite queues, link transit buffers, temperatures $T_{i,k}\in\{0,\ldots,63\}$, degradation $h_{i,k}\in\{0,1,2\}$, energy $e_k\in\{0,\ldots,64\}$, a common load $g_k\in\{0,1\}$, common sensor fault $F_k\in\{0,1\}$, delayed commands, and the absorbing failure flag.

Each queue has capacity sixteen and stores job identifiers, absolute deadlines, and whether the job was born during scoring. Identifiers and tie rules are public. Each link has three transit slots: at most one departure per link per step and delay at most two bound the outstanding occupancy. Arrival staging has capacity eighteen (a full queue plus at most two incoming jobs); first-layer staging needs at most seventeen. Command histories hold three steps, with one next-boundary latch. Auditor and sensor interfaces have the finite slots specified below. All these are physical world state, not freely observed controller state.

Draw the following persistent parameters independently for each node:

\[
\begin{aligned}
b_i&\sim\operatorname{Ber}(1/2),&z_i&\sim\operatorname{Ber}(1/4),\\
\rho_i&\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{4,8\},&\gamma_i&\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{2,3,4\},\\
\eta_i&\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{3,4,5\},&\lambda_i&\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{0,1,2\},\\
\beta_i^T&\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{-2,-1,0,1,2\},&
\beta_i^q&\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{-1,0,1\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{N1}
\]

Every link has independent fixed delay $d_{ij}\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{0,1,2\}$. Independently select an unordered pair $S$ of distinct nodes uniformly among the 120 pairs, and a scored change time

\[
\tau\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{1536,2048,2560\}.
\tag{N2}
\]

The change time and pair are part of the persistent world index. Nature cannot move the change to defeat the particular computation selected by the controller.

The public serialized task contract supplies this family and these priors, not their realizations. Robust evaluation ranges over every parameter realization in their support. Bayesian evaluation uses the displayed independent prior. Both must be reported separately.

### 7.2 Commands and actuator semantics

An accepted normal command specifies two binary channels and one route bit at every node. Route bits at last-layer nodes are fixed to zero. Let $u_i(k)$ be the command latched for boundary $k$, submitted during $[(k-1)\Delta,k\Delta)$. There is one pending normal latch per boundary; a later completed submission replaces that latch and still pays its complete interface cost. Without STOP or energy rejection, the channels and route applied in step $k$ are those of $u_i(k-\lambda_i)$. Negative indices use the initial zero command. A late submission is charged and rejected for its designated boundary; it is never rolled into a later command latch. STOP and energy rejection clear every stored channel and route command. Subsequent normal submissions refill the buffers; still-unfilled delayed positions remain zero. Buffers and their ages are hidden state.

Let $q_{i,k}^+$ be occupancy after arrival, expiration, and capacity enforcement at step $k$. Define

\[
\sigma_i(k)=b_i\oplus z_i\mathbf1\{q_{i,k}^+\ge\rho_i\}
\oplus\mathbf1\{i\in S,\ k_{\mathrm{score}}\ge\tau\},
\tag{N3}
\]

where the last indicator is zero before scoring begins. $k_{\mathrm{score}}$ counts scored steps starting at zero.

If the delayed channel pair is $(a_i^1,a_i^2)$, its physical outputs are

\[
(p_i,c_i)=
\begin{cases}
(a_i^1,a_i^2),&\sigma_i(k)=0,\\
(a_i^2,a_i^1),&\sigma_i(k)=1.
\end{cases}
\tag{N4}
\]

Here $p_i$ powers processing and $c_i$ powers cooling. The delayed route bit selects the outgoing link. Missing ordinary commands are replaced by the specified STOP watchdog at the next boundary, rather than holding an old risky command forever.

### 7.3 Exact transition order

The transition from boundary $k$ to $k+1$ has this order:

1. Insert link arrivals due at $k$. At each first-layer node draw a source arrival independently conditional on $g_k$, with probability $(1+g_k)/4$. A job born at $k$ has deadline $k+12$ and is eligible only while its processing index is strictly below that deadline. Remove expired jobs. If a queue exceeds sixteen, retain the sixteen earliest deadlines, breaking ties by identifier.
2. Compute (N3), apply delayed commands, and impose the energy interface below.
3. Process at most one earliest-deadline job at every node simultaneously. Conditional on the current state, its success probability when powered is
   \[
   \Pr(\chi_i=1\mid h_{i,k},g_k)=\frac{15-2h_{i,k}-g_k}{16}.
   \tag{N5}
   \]
   An unpowered or empty node completes no job. Processing noises are conditionally independent. Failed processing leaves the job queued.
4. A successful nonterminal completion leaves its queue and is scheduled to arrive at boundary $k+1+d_{ij}$. A last-layer on-time completion is a provisional physical completion. Scored utility counts it only if this same boundary has no failure, as specified in (N15). The simultaneous rule excludes multi-layer service within one step even when $d_{ij}=0$.
5. Update temperatures, degradation, common load, and sensor fault by (N6)–(N9). Set the absorbing failure flag if any updated temperature is at least 48.
6. Generate the timestamped observation packet. Its occupancy measurements refer to $q_{i,k}^+$ and its temperature measurements to $T_{i,k+1}$, as fixed by the interface definition.

Before actuator application, replenish energy to $e_k^+=\min(64,e_k+12)$. If requested demand $\sum_i(p_i+c_i)$ exceeds $e_k^+$, set every output to zero and clear pending command buffers. Otherwise subtract that demand. The resulting energy and rejection flag are observed in the packet. Energy therefore couples all node controls.

Temperatures satisfy

\[
T_{i,k+1}=\operatorname{clip}_{0,63}\left(
\left\lfloor\frac{4T_{i,k}+T_{i-1,k}+T_{i+1,k}}6\right\rfloor
+(\gamma_i+g_k)p_i-\eta_i c_i-1+\xi_{i,k}\right),
\quad
\xi_{i,k}\sim\operatorname{Ber}\!\left(\frac{1+g_k}{8}\right).
\tag{N6}
\]

For $U_{i,k}\sim\operatorname{Unif}\{0,\ldots,63\}$,

\[
h_{i,k+1}=\begin{cases}
\min(2,h_{i,k}+1),&U_{i,k}<1+g_k+p_i\mathbf1\{T_{i,k}\ge40\},\\
\max(0,h_{i,k}-1),&U_{i,k}\ge64-2c_i,\\
h_{i,k},&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\tag{N7}
\]

The two exceptional ranges are disjoint. Common load evolves by

\[
\Pr(g_{k+1}=1\mid g_k=0)=1/32,
\qquad\Pr(g_{k+1}=0\mid g_k=1)=1/8.
\tag{N8}
\]

The sensor fault evolves by

\[
\Pr(F_{k+1}=1\mid F_k=0,g_k)=(1+g_k)/256,
\qquad\Pr(F_{k+1}=0\mid F_k=1)=1/16.
\tag{N9}
\]

All draws at a step are independent conditional on the preceding state and persistent parameters, except for dependencies explicitly specified above. Their common parents create unconditional dependence.

### 7.4 Viability, timing, and failure

The active system starts with empty queues and link buffers, $T_i=16$, $h_i=0$, $e=64$, $F=0$, zero commands, and $g\sim\operatorname{Ber}(1/5)$. It has no reset interface.

STOP overrides its designated boundary's actuators with $p_i=c_i=0$, clears pending actuator commands, and rejects new processing commands until a later accepted normal command. Source jobs and deadlines continue to evolve. **Established STOP invariant.** If the maximum boundary temperature is $m<48$, the old-state diffusion average in (N6) is at most $m$. With $p=c=0$, the remaining increment is $-1+\xi\le0$. Clipping cannot raise that maximum. Induction gives no thermal failure after STOP applied before actuation. Every neighbor in (N6) is its old-state value; no sequential node update is allowed. At an intermediate microtime, account for any already-running step and its risk before STOP can take effect. This supplies a nonempty initial thermal-safe continuation without a hidden verifier; it does not undo a failure incurred during submission latency.

Once failure occurs, the flag remains one, the hardware enforces STOP, and subsequent active utility is zero. Physical cooling and observation can continue. A reset in a historical data-collection episode is not an active-system capability.

An interval $[k\Delta,(k+1)\Delta)$ is the computation and submission window for the next command boundary. The full command must finish submission before its deadline. A missed deadline invokes STOP. Code selection, search, archive transfer, and verification can therefore alter the physical control sequence even when they eventually derive the same policy.

The world has a finite phase counter between boundaries, so microtick evolution is a valid instance of (M). At boundary $k\ge1$, first mature packet $k-2$ if present, then finalize step $k-1$ and generate its pending packet, then begin step $k$ if $k<5120$. Boundary zero only begins step zero. No controller instruction occurs between these atomic phases. Interface acceptance exactly at a boundary sees the new step after those phases. All thermal neighbors use old values. It is not frozen by internal thinking.

### 7.5 Noisy delayed observations and a shared-blindness auditor

The sensor packet for step $k$ is generated at $(k+1)\Delta$ and becomes readable at $(k+2)\Delta$. One pending generation slot and one latest-readable slot suffice; publication overwrites the prior readable packet. Its measurements are

\[
Y_{i,k}^T=\operatorname{clip}_{0,31}
\bigl(\lfloor T_{i,k+1}/2\rfloor+\beta_i^T-3F_{k+1}+\epsilon_{i,k}^T\bigr),
\tag{N10}
\]

\[
Y_{i,k}^q=\operatorname{clip}_{0,8}
\bigl(\lfloor q_{i,k}^+/2\rfloor+\beta_i^q-F_{k+1}+\epsilon_{i,k}^q\bigr).
\tag{N11}
\]

Each $\epsilon$ has probabilities $1/16,7/8,1/16$ on $-1,0,1$, conditionally independently of other fresh noises. The biases and common fault remain shared latent variables.

The packet also records its event identifier, episode identifier, physical timestamp, source arrivals, latched requested command and mode (not its hidden delayed physical outputs), energy and rejection, on-time last-layer completion count, and failure flag. It contains no true temperature, degradation, common-load label, full queue state, or actuator orientation.

The live sensor interface retains only the latest readable packet. The overwritten packet is lost unless the controller copied it into charged memory. Packet availability is not free packet processing.

There is at most one pending auditor request. A query at node $i$ and boundary state $k$ returns two physical steps later:

\[
Z_j(i,k)=b_i\oplus(1-A)
\left(z_i\mathbf1\{q_{i,k}^+\ge\rho_i\}
\oplus\mathbf1\{i\in S,k_{\mathrm{score}}\ge\tau\}\right)\oplus E_j,
\tag{N12}
\]

where $A\sim\operatorname{Ber}(1/8)$ is one lifetime blind-spot variable shared across every node and call, and $E_j\stackrel{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\operatorname{Ber}(1/16)$. These variables are independent of initial parameters and physical noises. The returned correction is evaluated at invocation, not response time.

For an invocation accepted at microtime $r\in[k\Delta,(k+1)\Delta)$, use the stored arrival-processed snapshot $q_{i,k}^+$ and step index $k$. Acceptance means completion of the charged invocation interface. The result becomes ready at $r+2\Delta$ and retains that snapshot index. Its identifier encodes episode, acceptance microtick $r$, auditor event kind, and node; repeated canceled invocations within one physical step remain distinct. Its fresh error flip is drawn at acceptance and retained, so a later read cannot redraw it. Reading before readiness returns a charged rejection and no observation. An unread ready result occupies the single request slot until retrieval or cancellation. Cancellation discards it, costs 128 interface units and microticks, and creates no response evidence. The lifetime variable $A$ is drawn once before archive generation and active operation and belongs to the persistent world index. Robust evaluation includes both its values. Historical resets retain $A$ and all node/link parameters.

The auditor is a delayed evidence channel, not a proof of orientation. A response can become obsolete before it arrives.

### 7.6 Historical archive and physical memory

The initial archive contains 1,024 historical episodes of 64 steps, sharing the active system's persistent node and link parameters. The structural change is inactive in these episodes. Their reset state is the active initial state above, with a fresh $g\sim\operatorname{Ber}(1/5)$ in each episode and fresh physical noise. A record is the raw packet generated by a historical transition, captured by that experiment's declared logger at generation, not the delayed packet then available to a controller. Exactly sixty-four records are generated per episode. At reset, clear all delayed packets, commands, transit buffers, and pending diagnostic state. No delayed event crosses a reset. The active system retains its one-step sensor delay.

At each historical step choose uniformly exactly twelve of the thirty-two channels to set to one, and choose nonterminal routing bits independently and fairly. After a historical failure enforce STOP until its scheduled reset; those records have zero command payload rather than a twelve-channel normal command. Terminal route bits are always zero. Historical resets are declared experimental interventions and appear in provenance.

Each of the 65,536 records occupies 512 bits. Use this exact layout: 64-bit event identifier; 16-bit episode identifier; 32-bit physical index; 4 arrival bits; 48 accepted channel-and-route bits; 1 mode bit; 7 energy bits; 1 rejection bit; 3 completion-count bits; 1 failure bit; 64 occupancy-measurement bits; 80 temperature-measurement bits; and 191 fixed zero padding bits. Public interface rules determine timestamps and reset interpretation. No hidden-state labels are included.

The three-bit completion field records all on-time last-layer completions, including background jobs. The evaluator separately selects scored-birth jobs for utility. Historical data are a supplied initial experiment, not free inference-time acquisition: no new historical episodes or reset access are available to the active controller.

The archive is 4 MiB, divided into 4 KiB pages. A full-page open and transfer costs 2,560 interface-work units and microticks, including its invocation. The 2,048-unit page-opening cost is paid even for a partial transfer; each transferred 64-bit word costs one additional unit. The actual transfer buffer counts toward peak memory. Reading the same record again does not create another independent trial.

Erased archive storage can be reclassified as ordinary writable memory only through paid erasure and allocation. Treat physical cold-store regions as opaque until accessed, even if their addresses are known.

### 7.7 Hardware costs and the reference regime

Use 64-bit instructions and sixteen 64-bit registers. An ordinary bounded-width instruction costs one processor-work unit and one microtick, including fetch and execution. Wide arithmetic expands into instructions. The declared I/O stalls are sequential; processor and environment clocks are not paused.

| Interface | Processor/interface work accounting | Physical duration |
|---|---:|---:|
| Sensor packet read | 72 declared interface units | 72 microticks |
| Complete command submission | 65 declared interface units | 65 microticks |
| Auditor invocation | 128 declared interface units | 128 microticks, then two-step asynchronous response delay |
| Auditor result retrieval | 128 declared interface units | 128 microticks |
| Full cold page transfer | 2,560 declared interface units | 2,560 microticks |
| Prediction packet submission | 6 declared interface units | 6 microticks |
| Autonomous deadline watchdog | 1 reserved interface unit per active step | 1 reserved microtick per active step |

These costs include invoking instructions. An explicit STOP uses the 65-unit command-submission interface. The watchdog's fixed implementation belongs to $L_0$; reserve its 5,120 work units before allocating the remaining active budget. Its microtick lies within each $\Delta$ interval. Ordinary submissions must finish no later than the start of that reserved final tick. If discretionary work runs out after a normal command has already been accepted, that pending command still applies at its target boundary. Subsequent missing commands invoke STOP; exhaustion cannot erase the risk of the already accepted command. Reserve and guard against that exposure. All application-level decoding, likelihood propagation, program construction, proof checking, and selection are additional processor work. In this benchmark the scalar work budget is the sum of the two declared work ledgers; report the components separately as well.

Use

\[
\boxed{
\Delta=8192,\quad T_0=2^{23},\quad H_{\mathrm{score}}=4096,
\quad B=2^{25},\quad\delta=2^{-20}.}
\tag{N13}
\]

Preparation lasts 1,024 physical steps. The active scoring horizon lasts a further 4,096, with scored processing indices $k=1024,\ldots,5119$. Thus total active physical time is $5120\Delta=41{,}943{,}040$ microticks. Background jobs during preparation affect state but do not count toward score. All sensor and diagnostic latency consumes this same physical horizon.

Let $L_0$ be the declared initial-image and fixed-index storage size, bounded and selected before parameters. Set

\[
M=65{,}536\cdot512+L_0+16\cdot64+262{,}144
\quad\text{bits}.
\tag{N14}
\]

The additional writable allowance is 32 KiB. An implicit posterior array, compiler workspace, proof cache, model ensemble, or unbounded telemetry log is excluded. The contents counted in $L_0$ cannot include an experiment-specific solution or parameter realization.

Require $L_0\le2^{20}$ bits, selected before hidden parameters. Completion produced by processing step $k$ occurs at boundary $k+1$. It is on time exactly when $k+1\le d_{\mathrm{job}}$. Score only jobs born at $k=1024,\ldots,5119$, completed by boundary 5120 on time, and with $D_{k+1}=0$; a completion at the first failing boundary is excluded:

\[
G=\frac{\#\{\text{scored-birth, on-time completions with }D_{k+1}=0\}}{4H_{\mathrm{score}}}.
\tag{N15}
\]

For scored step $k$, forecasts of its four source-arrival indicators and sixteen indicators $\mathbf1\{T_{i,k+1}\ge40\}$ must be committed before boundary $k$, before its arrivals are revealed. They cannot be revised after observing those arrivals. Reports use the finite grid $\{r/256:0\le r\le256\}$, encoded in nine bits each. The prediction frame contains the twenty reports plus a 64-bit event identifier and 32-bit timestamp, occupies five 64-bit words with 44 fixed zero padding bits (276 payload bits), and uses the six-unit interface. The normalized Brier loss averages over all $20\cdot4096=81{,}920$ fixed events, including targets after failure. Report the exogenous arrival loss and controlled thermal loss separately. Missing reports use $1/2$. Prediction construction, representation, and arithmetic are additional charged work. Approximation and asymptotic claims must respect this fixed reporting precision unless a different task family explicitly changes it.

The numerical regime is a specified finite stress case, not an assertion that an optimal controller or useful certificate fits its budget. A solution must determine feasibility, value, and approximation gaps. If a proposed mechanism cannot fit this regime, prove its resource threshold and analyze adjacent scalable regimes rather than hiding the cost.

### 7.8 Initial misspecification

The initial active modeling language contains stationary independently factorized node models with constant actuator mapping and independent diagnostic errors. It omits common latent load, common fault, occupancy-triggered switching, delayed command state, shared auditor blindness, and structural change.

The serialized public generator can be read and translated into a richer causal program through paid execution. Its fixed encoding occupies a declared read-only portion of $L_0$; these bytes count toward the initial-image limit and memory, and every runtime load and parse is charged. It is not an extra uncounted input region. It supplies structural information, not hidden realizations. Acknowledging that this structural information is public is essential: a model-family promise known only to the analyst cannot justify the executable controller's confidence.

Until richer structure is installed and its inference approximation is checked, outer uncertainty retains the omitted mechanisms. A false factorized model must not receive a coverage certificate merely because it is fast.

### 7.9 Coupled analysis and comparison obligations

Use the one executed law (M) for robust and Bayesian evaluations, with the same global ledger, contract rules, and resource profile. Establish six quantitative results:

1. Feasibility and useful value under STOP reserve, delayed commands, hidden orientations, coupled energy, and a global failure target of $2^{-20}$; report certificate and computation thresholds where the reference regime cannot support them.
2. Identifiability under safe asymmetric interventions, with nonidentifiable combinations stated. Diagonal controls have orientation-independent physical outputs only under a fixed input protocol or an adaptive policy whose information channels do not disclose orientation; asymmetric archive and auditor information invalidate an unrestricted passive-impossibility claim.
3. Bounds or counterexamples for independent-node, independent-auditor, fixed-orientation, and delay-free approximations, carrying their utility, risk, and recovery effects through the full physical prefix.
4. Constructive comparison of raw retention, finite-family sufficient summaries, approximate filters, and point estimates, identifying which later model expansions and interventions each preserves.
5. Real code-specialization advantage or harm, including discovery, proof production, installation, peak memory, destroyed evidence, pending events, and changed command timing. Equal current actions need not imply equal future feasible profiles.
6. Hidden-shift detection limits and recovery curves, including shifts concealed by diagonal operation, safe excitation costs, and uncertainty remaining after a stale or blind auditor response.

Every baseline must have actual published code, cost accounting, and information restrictions. The requested strict separation must occur in a proved nonempty regime; no mechanism is assumed useful in every regime.

## 8. Exact lower bounds that constrain the architecture

The parity-exception experiment is retained as a lower-bound subfamily, not as a substitute for the dynamical network. Let $n\ge3$, $N=2^n$, and independent uniform $s,\kappa\in\{0,1\}^n$. Set

\[
d(v)=\langle s,v\rangle_{\mathbb F_2}\oplus\mathbf1\{v=\kappa\}.
\tag{L1}
\]

For fresh $U\sim\operatorname{Ber}(1/2)$, $d(v)=0$ means $P=U,Q=P$, and $d(v)=1$ means $Q=U,P=Q$. Both produce the same passive pair $(U,U)$. In the first world $\operatorname{do}(P=1)$ ensures $Q=1$; in the second, $\operatorname{do}(Q=1)$ ensures $P=1$. The opposite intervention fails with probability $1/2$.

A charged diagnostic isolates the device from the scored target and cannot cause deployment failure. A scored intervention actuates that target; its failed outcome is absorbing. This is a declared physical separation, not a free oracle. Every archive read, diagnostic, intervention, and delayed feedback has an event identifier and enters the paid transcript.

The archive provides $R=n+24$ independent historical $\operatorname{do}(P=1)$ outcomes at each context. The auditor returns

\[
Z_j(v)=\langle s,v\rangle_{\mathbb F_2}\oplus(1-A)\mathbf1\{v=\kappa\}\oplus E_j,
\quad A\sim\operatorname{Ber}(1/8),\quad E_j\sim\operatorname{Ber}(1/16),
\tag{L2}
\]

with one persistent $A$ and independent fresh flips. Initial active prediction code expresses parity only; a branch, cache, or generated routine implementing the exception is an expansion regardless of its name.

Use instruction width $W=4+3\lceil\log_2(8n)\rceil$. An instruction costs $W$ work and ticks; an archive page costs $(512+R)W$, a diagnostic $32W$, an auditor call $4W$, and input, deployment, and post-deployment feedback each $W$. Each of $K$ uniform-context opportunities lasts at most $80W$ ticks, and independently closes per tick with probability $1/(64\cdot80W)$. A late, closed, or absent deployment yields zero utility and no target failure. Feedback arrives only after deployment. Active failure sets all subsequent utility to zero.

Retain $n=12$, $R=36$, $K=256$, and global risk $2^{-16}$. The declared generic evaluator takes $(10n+4)W=124W$, exceeding a window; the specified specialized evaluator takes

\[
J(s,\kappa)W=[3n+\operatorname{wt}(s)-\operatorname{wt}(\kappa)+4]W,
\quad 28\le J\le52.
\tag{L3}
\]

This is a cost contract requiring emitted bytecode, a correctness proof, and a paid producer. It is not a claim that specialization has already been implemented. Preparation, discovery, proof, installation, and missed opportunities belong in (M). Let $M=\ell_{\mathrm{arc}}+L_0+16W+s_0$, where the actual archive encoding includes provenance and $s_0$ is the variable writable allowance.

**Established access lemma.** Grant $s$ as analytical side information and condition on $A=1$. If at most $q$ distinct contexts are touched on every execution through any informative real channel, then

\[
\Pr(\widehat\kappa=\kappa)\le\min\{1,(q+1)/N\}.
\tag{L4}
\]

Proof: strengthen each touched context to noiseless disclosure of whether it equals $\kappa$, which dominates the actual data. Fix private coins and consider the all-negative query path. It tests at most $q$ distinct points; before discovery, nothing distinguishes the remaining points. Its final guess contributes at most one more point. Thus at most $q+1$ of the $N$ equally likely exceptions succeed. Average over private coins. The same bound applies with noisy archive and diagnostic outcomes by simulating them from the stronger disclosures and independent noise. Auditor calls at $A=1$ provide no exception information. Every informative deployment feedback counts as a touched context.

For a random count $Q$ and deterministic cutoff $r$, truncating the strengthened experiment gives

\[
\Pr(\widehat\kappa=\kappa)\le\min\{1,(r+1)/N+\Pr(Q>r)\}.
\tag{L5}
\]

Adaptive stopping does not justify substituting $\mathbb E Q$ into (L4).

**Established exact-retention lemma.** A fixed retained representation that answers every context exactly, without another informative external read, requires at least $2n$ bits of joint variable code and data. There are $2^{2n}$ distinct functions (L1). If two were equal with $s\ne s'$, their parity difference would have support $N/2\ge4$, whereas the difference of two singleton exceptions has support at most two. Thus $s=s'$ and then $\kappa=\kappa'$. Exact decodable representations must distinguish all functions. This proves the bit count, not the availability of a fast decoder. Safe abstention on uncertain contexts can use less memory; derive its separate risk/utility/coverage frontier.

For repeated auditor queries at a fixed exceptional context, the majority limit disagrees with the true orientation exactly when $A=1$, giving error $1/8$ under the stated prior. This is a repeated-auditor error floor at that context, not a lower bound for controllers that also obtain physical diagnostic evidence. Ignoring the singleton can give prediction accuracy $1-1/N$ while retaining an unsafe response on that point. Derive its actual multi-window risk with the absorbing and closure rules, rather than multiplying independent failure probabilities after a first failure.

Use these results to constrain archive access, retained exceptions, and correlated verification in the network. Prove any network transfer through its actual interface; resemblance alone is not a reduction.

## 9. What counts as a successful computer implementation

The mathematical objective is now operational: publish one image $\Sigma$ and show that its **executed** laws achieve a positive simultaneous improvement margin on a fixed harness while meeting resources and the actual risk limits. Solving the design supremum, or enumerating its finite domain, is not assumed affordable.

Pre-register the task versions, priors or robust supports, baseline images, information access, hardware/cost profiles, utility scales, risk targets, data split, controller hash, interpreter hash, compiler environment, seeds, and confidence allocation. Hold out full world realizations: repeated steps or auditor calls within one lifetime are dependent samples. Give baseline programs the same declared information, data, and resources unless the comparison explicitly studies a restriction.

For $J$ fixed tasks, let $\underline u_j$ and $\overline u_j^{\mathrm{base}}$ be jointly valid utility bounds at the committed confidence level. The demonstrated margin is

\[
\underline\gamma=\min_{j\le J}
\frac{\underline u_j-\overline u_j^{\mathrm{base}}}{s_j}.
\tag{V}
\]

Require $\underline\gamma>0$ and the separately justified safety/resource constraints. A paired design can improve precision; it must account for common noise and baseline uncertainty. Bayesian episode averages do not certify robust all-support safety or worst-case utility. Publish failed and infeasible regimes as well as wins.

Zero observed failures is weak evidence at rare-risk targets. For $n$ independent identically distributed full episodes and zero failures, the exact one-sided upper bound at confidence $1-\beta$ is $1-\beta^{1/n}$. At $\beta=0.05$ and target $2^{-20}$, the required count is

\[
n\ge\left\lceil\frac{\log0.05}{\log(1-2^{-20})}\right\rceil.
\tag{RISK-TEST}
\]

The bundled validator prints the numerical count. Individual network steps are not interchangeable with independent full episodes. A formal finite certificate or a validated rare-event estimator can support a different route; its assumptions and actual controller costs must be published.

Treat six validation levels separately: specification consistency; interpreter and interface conformance; finite certificate soundness; resource-correct controller execution; task-specific utility/risk evidence; and cross-task transfer. The included JSON and validator reach the first level for the checks they actually run. They do not implement the controller, establish all structural simulator semantics, or provide measured benchmark wins.

A complete solution must supply the finite instruction decoder, actual bytecode image, producer/selector/checker/dispatcher, object encodings, versioned contract rules, trace hashes, and resource schedules. Prove NI, EF, conditional coverage, contract composition, and fallback feasibility. Use the supplied e-factor and risk-ledger lemmas with task-specific premises. Quantify proof feasibility, access, latency, retained information, numerical enclosure, and nonrectangular uncertainty losses. Establish quantitative improvement against precisely restricted baselines in nonempty regimes and state its limits.

The benchmark cannot be replaced by independent one-step decisions, perfect-state observation, or a free simulator. A fixed universal interpreter can emulate adaptive code: a claimed architectural separation must identify its actual resource or information restriction. Prediction loss on controlled thermal targets is action-dependent, so a STOP policy can simplify that target. Compare causal predictors under a fixed intervention protocol; report exogenous arrival prediction separately. Missing forecasts use $1/2$, which can outperform a poor report and save work. Do not claim that omission can never help.

## 10. Attribution and research status

This revision supplies an executed-law equation, consistent finite-law benchmark, concrete proof lemmas, and an inspectable computer representation. Full controller synthesis, task-specific certificates, and benchmark performance remain required results. The equation is an ambitious specification of bounded adaptive intelligence; no accepted mathematical formula alone establishes superintelligence.

Established ingredients are supported by these primary sources; the combined architecture and its proposed performance are not attributed to them:

- Howard, Ramdas, McAuliffe, and Sekhon, [Time-uniform, nonparametric, nonasymptotic confidence sequences](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.08240), for time-uniform inference.
- Goyal and Grand-Clément, [Robust Markov Decision Process: Beyond Rectangularity](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.00215), for coupled uncertainty beyond independently reselected transitions.
- Leroy, [Formal verification of a realistic compiler](https://xavierleroy.org/publi/compcert-CACM.pdf), for semantic preservation of compiled code.
- Russell and Wefald, [Principles of Metareasoning](https://iiif.library.cmu.edu/file/Newell_box00014_fld01011_doc0001/Newell_box00014_fld01011_doc0001.pdf), for computation selection as a decision problem.


