--[============[
asi/guest/pkg.lua -- runtime code packages: producer, checkers, installer, dispatcher support.

OBJECT. The planner's decision kernel maps per node (qb in 0..31, tb in 0..9, constants: ORIOK_i and the
parameter table PAR) to a class/style code DS_i. The GENERIC kernel is table driven. A PACKAGE is a
straight-line specialisation of the same function emitted at run time as VM-1 instruction WORDS into a
code buffer: parameters and per-node orientation validity become immediates, so it executes fewer
instructions. It is installed only if every check below passes, it is CURRENT, and the paid cost-benefit
rule says the saving over the remaining windows exceeds the metered cost of producing and checking it.
Payback is a model-conditional work estimate: it extrapolates the measured current-input saving, less
dispatch overhead, and also requires a positive measured mean over the complete uniform 320-point grid.
The grid minimum is retained separately; semantic equivalence proves behavior, not future input frequencies
or a universal positive timing saving. A timing estimate is never a safety certificate.

PRODUCER (resident, charged): a micro-code interpreter. Two row tables (variant A: diagonal only; variant B:
single channel allowed) describe each node's block; rows carry (template word, immediate kind, value).
Emission = a loop over nodes and rows; each emitted word is template | (imm & 2^44-1).
The default lookup producer first computes two 320-word tables through bounded paid VM slices, one for
each orientation-validity case. It emits eight instructions per node: two input loads, QB & 31, TB % 10,
multiply/add to form the index, a lookup load, and its own DS store. These masks preserve every declared
input and keep even malformed 64-bit inputs inside the table. package_lookup=false retains the original
branch-template producer. Lookup tables are regenerated only while no installed package is active.

STATIC CHECKER (resident, charged) - a finite sound check of a declared language ("sandboxed straight-line
kernel"). Accepts only: LD/ST with the zero register as base (r0 == 0 for the whole run), MOVI, SLTI, ADDI,
BEQZ, BNEZ, and one final JR r15; destination registers r1..r4 only; every branch strictly forward and
inside its own node block; node block i reads only QB[i], TB[i] and writes only DS[i]; every block ends
with its DS[i] store; block table strictly increasing from 0 to the final JR. Consequences (proved by the
checks): no interface instruction, no coin, no write outside DS, no write to r0/r14/r15, termination in at
most len instructions (acyclic), per-node independence. Anything else is REJECTED, never ignored.
The lookup checker instead requires each complete instruction word to match that exact eight-word shape,
with its own QB/TB/DS addresses and one of the two dedicated table bases. The table version must match
the captured premises, all blocks have length eight, and the final word is exactly JR r15. The index
lies in 0..319 for every 64-bit input, so there are no unrestricted indexed loads. Provenance hashes cover
all emitted code and all 640 table words; the same full-domain semantic checker remains mandatory.

SEMANTIC CHECKER: because node i is independent and its inputs are (qb, tb) in a FINITE product domain of
32 x 10 = 320 points, running the generic kernel and the package on every point with all nodes set to the
same input proves equality of the two functions on their entire domain for the current PAR/ORIOK.
(An exhaustive finite derivation: bit-vector/enumeration fact, not sampling.) It runs in bounded slices.

VERSIONS / INVALIDATION. A package records the parameter version PARV it was produced and checked for.
The dispatcher uses it only while PKGVER == PARV; any parameter change or orientation change bumps PARV, so
the package is stale and the generic kernel runs (no proof is reused across versions). Re-checking is
charged. Rollback: PKGACT is a single word; two code slots mean a failed stage never touches the live one.
]============]
